#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050790 :topic/math-academy-id 95 :topic/title "Graphing Linear Equations"}] :topic/id #uuid "b412ece5-8f53-54cb-9936-346283210a0b" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138428 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138083 :question/id #uuid "cbf28a0c-c34d-5855-b72e-4225a11710c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13129" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n${x + 2y = 8; 2x - y = 6$\n\nUse the diagram below to determine this system's solution $(x,y)$.\n\n![](images/6f/6f0b7960b9b4c98250741b009da2ad19c1123c5b7adf9cd5072430fb9a879dc9.png)" :question/worked-solution "The solution of the system corresponds to the point of intersection of the lines.\n\n![](images/5c/5c1c69adc880ea7cfdb5a4e0a10abd6032d2eedf0386923b94df9b7c173edd6d.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(4,2)$. Therefore, the solution to the given system is $(4,2)$."} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a System From Its Graph"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138429 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138084 :question/id #uuid "86771ced-afc0-5061-a532-bc2345a52ea8" :question/math-academy-id "e-13130" :question/problem "![](images/09/0915c99015922e27855d92ebb13756da634a8f10dd8fe3abbc016590f06e1077.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the number of liters of water in pool $A$ and pool $B$ as a function of time, measured in days. How many days will it take for both pools to contain the same amount of water?" :question/worked-solution "The pools have the same amount of water when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/cf/cf7a9b8bdccbc66086c473813523ed893ee192830965300a8c4a0702ad065fc3.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(5,400)$. So, both pools have the same amount ($400\\,\\textrm{L}$) of water at $t=5$ days."} :knowledge-point/title "Word Problems"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2225 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826 :topic/math-academy-id 487 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Equations by Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049060 :topic/math-academy-id 742 :topic/title "Introduction to the Elimination Method"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049330 :topic/math-academy-id 101 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Nonlinear Equations Using Graphs"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050263 :topic/math-academy-id 136 :topic/title "Approximating Solutions to Systems of Linear Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050641 :topic/math-academy-id 1535 :topic/title "Systems of Linear Inequalities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765 :topic/math-academy-id 148 :topic/title "Systems of Equations as Augmented Matrices"}] :topic/title "Introduction to Systems of Linear Equations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048697 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049380 :topic/math-academy-id 1061 :topic/title "Solving First-Order ODEs Using Direct Integration"}] :topic/id #uuid "b5d86240-2aa3-5be2-b9a5-9ed16336f711" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068840 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068447 :question/id #uuid "d1de6657-82b1-5bcb-b8cc-fd2629d31faa" :question/math-academy-id "e-10832" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' = \\dfrac {x^2}{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y^2 = \\dfrac{1}{3}x^3 + C.\n$$\n\nWe can express the solution in the form $y = y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+2C \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+2C} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+K}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = 2C.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Explicit Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068448 :question/id #uuid "23d66cc8-8e5a-5a33-a7a1-c43f5facdcbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10833" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' + 2xy = 0$ for $y\\neq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2xy \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\ln \\vert y \\vert = -x^2 + C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y = y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = e^C$  and $K > 0.$ \n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ke^{-x^2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions by Rearranging Then Integrating"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068449 :question/id #uuid "32040154-373e-5a10-a460-1d17f6989da0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10849" :question/problem "Given that $x,y \\geq 0,$ separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2xy}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{g(y)} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =  \\sqrt{2x} \\cdot  \\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2x}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Separating the Variables in a Differential Equation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068844 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068451 :question/id #uuid "d53c6172-978d-57c7-b1f0-e56e892e48a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10997" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    \\dfrac{x}{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+K \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+2K \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C= 2K.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\ny^2 = x^2 + C .\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Implicit Form"}] :topic/math-academy-id 466 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048894 :topic/math-academy-id 1824 :topic/title "Orthogonal Trajectories"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049102 :topic/math-academy-id 1925 :topic/title "Reduction of Order"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049562 :topic/math-academy-id 3179 :topic/title "Solving First-Order ODEs by Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050905 :topic/math-academy-id 1179 :topic/title "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Separation of Variables"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050995 :topic/math-academy-id 878 :topic/title "Homogeneous First-Order ODEs"}] :topic/title "Solving First-Order ODEs Using Separation of Variables"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048789 :topic/math-academy-id 1530 :topic/title "Graphing Exponential Growth Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049589 :topic/math-academy-id 818 :topic/title "The Natural Logarithm"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711 :topic/math-academy-id 332 :topic/title "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line Using Differentiation"}] :topic/id #uuid "bad8d582-b827-501b-8a06-58b93e5ecdb0" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096610 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096410 :question/id #uuid "8e4367b2-f7bc-5588-b30b-d612915e3e68" :question/math-academy-id "e-3624" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3e^x + \\ln 3$, find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of $e^x$ is just $e^x$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln 3$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3e^x + \\ln 3\\right)\\\n                                                  &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( 3e^x\\right) +  \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( \\ln 3 \\right)\\\n                                                  &= 3\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right) + 0\\\n                                                  &= 3 e^x.\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096611 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096411 :question/id #uuid "48334c0c-a49e-5dfb-9f8a-8caa0c22d1f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3625" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=4e^x+ 3x^4 - x^2,$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{ \n                          f'(x)             &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4e^x+3x^{4} - x^2 \\right)\\\n                                             &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4e^x\\right)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3x^{4}\\right) -\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^{2}\\right) \\\n\t\t\t\t\t     &= 4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right)+3\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^{4}\\right) - 2x \\\t\t\t\n                                             &=  4 e^x+  3 \\cdot 4x^{3} - 2x \\\n                                             &=  4 e^x+  12x^{3} - 2x.\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Polynomial"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096612 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096412 :question/id #uuid "16b1a91c-ae83-5e6b-a11d-bb9d78e4d218" :question/math-academy-id "e-3626" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3 \\cdot 2^x$, find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf'(x)      &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3 \\cdot 2^x\\right) \\\n                            &= 3 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(2^x\\right) \\\n                            &=  3 \\cdot 2^{x}\\ln(2) \\\n                            &=  3 \\ln(2) \\cdot 2^{x}.\n\t}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Base Other Than e"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1114 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271 :topic/math-academy-id 1007 :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Exponential Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690 :topic/math-academy-id 1116 :topic/title "Differentiating Logarithmic Functions"}] :topic/title "Differentiating Exponential Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048780 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050467 :topic/math-academy-id 1102 :topic/title "Problem Solving Using Vector Diagrams"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051014 :topic/math-academy-id 1105 :topic/title "Linear Combinations of Vectors and Their Properties"}] :topic/id #uuid "be10c23c-41f3-53b5-9245-d50fb098c325" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096451 :question/id #uuid "dccb5bac-e528-5b42-9949-36b3870fdde1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4287" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](images/b8/b8abf22d14f93a38c4d01f368b8171e4ccfe90086d932f5b58453baa46391405.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/76/76b9867a7c1ef484922241ebe44272f0104e406c31310bf36227a13850f50d8b.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2|\\mathbf{n}|$;\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}$.\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\overset{\\to }{ON}+\\overset{\\to }{NM} \\\\  & =2n+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}$. \n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096653 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096452 :question/id #uuid "09d593cc-526e-5691-9a18-efff515eae59" :question/math-academy-id "e-4288" :question/problem "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b},$ and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}.$\n\n![](images/67/678ea2936b8a5b1ded74cb8a0875c629e8be0f7002c98492ebf2e8109fc603ef.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}.$\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AX} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{3}{4}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}a+\\frac{3}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096495 :question/id #uuid "839ed5f5-165f-5de5-aba1-551ac605475f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5212" :question/problem "Consider the picture below.\n\n![](images/f2/f2518c2e998e7c7f551548a4cc7283720a66428ea8fa9872494a81cc3848ba9e.png)\n\nGiven that ![](images/c6/c6714d0a6eaa5c7d354d46e8d6d60da7f3d2011df0fc75773036cf7ae510c5e3.png), find ![](images/24/24878591902884f45f14f5c447bc326586a411a8870b205fc12825a4ab2c738a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when two known vectors are not parallel, a linear combination of them is unique. So, once we rewrite ![](images/5c/5c644b15efcf5a4274de23242a722604ead2ff800ea29ba7a650729227fefdf0.png) in terms of ![](images/27/27f36b1d1846e12e3c50b7d4e47f5745727792d52a48e4d85b689ec36e3d25a0.png) and ![](images/a7/a7b9033c421072576aaf959980d778c319a8ed0f849e08db644b63bc6994088c.png) we can match coefficients to determine the unknown scalar.\n\nTo find ![](images/16/166c90a9c29f99347bff6b397f31d705ec6407a19b4d71246f98308bbd7a5473.png) we want to match the expression\n![](images/fc/fc79367916dd7671c17de4f40b3d83f91d41c2f9988645964de0b53e5b11ab9b.png)\n\nWe are given that one of the components is ![](images/91/91f686c6ca02e85c386cb0f9e80b66beface446d3a541952e0ac91738ad0db37.png) So, we first draw the vector ![](images/d5/d54b9a8919c7b6106fcf5770c62b1823605d7be98a55d5333e73bcfb7528997a.png) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](images/6e/6e1bbbe9eb66856824ff70d16cc2c077519d361546b226a67146e20f15a24dd0.png)\n\n![](images/ea/ea517ac4ca8e0bcf7ba48f23e66f100323d5485f1221219aacfbea9148f4a1af.png)\n\nNote that in ![](images/f1/f1c944e6afa4e9cd84bc68004a2bfd918d81498a727aeb7c56ec627806bf93f6.png), we have:\n\n- ![](images/b7/b7ac390c4e43b2fa2fbb4063632f98869256cea877caa5e5dad4aad33ae94722.png)\n\n- ![](images/7e/7e5f2b01647ab01052fff18ab760273eebe19d47b56051b1953adf587d7f9fb3.png) and ![](images/78/784225d11bf94b7fbd67f8e2395695141eb75b1cb44f89d49b29bb3a2031534b.png) have the same direction\n\nHence, ![](images/21/21261c8ad0d86a9e7300ffc58c57fd76beef9f627492bcfe2051d5e3cfd82a4e.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n![](images/ae/ae9c98ded3aa4317a729571710ad38d973a2d67b5faeb9ad26af7c24ee5c3193.png)\n\nConsequently, ![](images/ae/aee3287ad64a654880216142756811bd4d5232b3dba2f48378b8b1c5241e4316.png)\n\nSince ![](images/46/466863f8c7522ac89896a4233a823be02940535097c28328fa2c9e9e1f4bc3d8.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](images/37/3720c5887713d3f7a29be384fe5e883390935b2b9b469af626a8dba968f4da8b.png) we get:\n![](images/4a/4ac145b710c5354b44a4ea1dc820d70f54b5c17985f078fce6313b671ec26fd9.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234976 :question/id #uuid "aeaa36a2-41b5-5b7b-9797-f39df1e34050" :question/math-academy-id "e-26144" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d4/d4f18c3567d0c55d3d6e002fbe2c6ac50b97b7adc14b016c8e95006de98ca15d.png)\n\nGiven that ![](images/a7/a77e5fef143d51d984ce41363465af5f210291f6355b59da4302a944b6a49197.png) find ![](images/8a/8aef212f0b07b6c0fb04e3b3c0e415f3c93d698d48414bc0860a6405db20099b.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find ![](images/ae/ae4e47577b23025037443e79415dc50ff81b984cac5c4bb82421f985cfe4d69b.png) we want to match the expression\n![](images/bd/bd1ef582b76affe848465f42a9c78e897d93f1a2b535b7e880da8ab016561451.png)\n\nWe are given that one of the components is ![](images/dc/dc10081b11a8cfba96ed4d16d9ca1c6448af7f6198a5550955c2d123a657d339.png) So, we first draw the vector ![](images/7e/7efe1d8a24967d9509e73d84e1fb73eea1df1c2f113360cd3a34d8ef29df62e9.png) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](images/06/06046901b5ecb50bfedb17ffaf4c19cf690e50bd2e6e7c668fa59787ce4d71b7.png)\n\n![](images/65/65a6b5743ab9b6ef820744d0d6601b7dfd8de08a3f895661d96d2fe2cab8e0a7.png)\n\nLet ![](images/49/492123d15daf8306f0f5f893c7230d60f9eb8312d17b20f9a331f40f36b69b48.png) be the endpoint of the vector ![](images/d8/d8c5a62b30eeb1bc2e35d95dfc5d964dd00e512f3f7b1b53379755b1ebff5129.png) Then\n\n![](images/2d/2d7c2df783579edf0ca151150eeb55471b3f3fc2